  "explanation": "Requested kinematics (W = 1.6 GeV, Q\u00b2 = 1.0 GeV\u00b2, cos\u03b8* = 0, 25 \u03c6* points spanning 0\u2013345\u00b0) are physically valid for eta electroproduction: W = 1.6 GeV is well above the p\u03b7 threshold (\u2248 1.4915 GeV) and inside the EtaMAID-2023 table range (W = 1.4856\u20136 GeV, Q\u00b2 = 0\u20135 GeV\u00b2 per mpintp.inc), and cos\u03b8* = 0 is inside the physical [-1, 1] range. All 4 kinematic arrays (W, Q\u00b2, cos\u03b8*, \u03c6*) have exactly N = 25 entries as required. \u03c6* values are a uniform 25-point grid from 0\u00b0 to 345\u00b0 inclusive (step = 14.375\u00b0) exactly as specified in the request. Held-fixed W and Q\u00b2 values are repeated 25 times to match array length, following the convention shown in the repo's own multi-point examples (input_single_chunk.dat, input_multi_chunk.dat). Only one input file was needed: the exclurad.F Fortran reader uses list-directed I/O (`read(5,*)(w_input(i),i=1,npoi)`), which is whitespace-delimited and continues across line breaks until npoi tokens are consumed (array dimension npoimax=10000, line 18 of exclurad.F). The README's statement that N is 'limited to 10 per file' is a convention used by the batch/Slurm runner scripts (which stage one input.dat at a time), not an actual limitation of the Fortran parser -- this is confirmed by the repo's own input_multi_chunk.dat example, which packs N = 50 points into wrapped 10-column rows in a single file for one exclurad.exe invocation. Following that same convention, the 25-point arrays here are wrapped as two full rows of 10 values plus one row of 5 values per kinematic variable, with the standard eta-channel defaults (model=3 maid2000/EtaMAID, ivec=1 recoil proton, bmom=6.53 GeV CLAS12 beam momentum, vcut=0.166 GeV\u00b2 CLAS12 inelasticity cut, rc_mode=0 full O(alpha) correction) taken from eta/input.dat and eta/examples/. No physical corrections were required; the request maps directly onto a single valid input file with no multi-file chunking needed."
